 ],
 "literal": "Listen, may you live long, friend! At all / if one says fault, is it a fault? There is none indeed! In the hot desert, / the dry-leaf-covered cairns of perished wayfarers / become the casting shade for the tall, fine elephants. / To him who went to that difficult desert forest, / my broad, soft shoulders became easy (wasted).",
 "translation": "Listen, my friend, may you live long! If you say there is any fault on his part, is it really a fault? No, there is none. In the hot desert, where the leaf-covered stone cairns of perished wayfarers offer the only shade to tall, fine elephants, he has gone. To him who journeyed into that difficult wilderness, my broad, soft shoulders have become so easy to leave behind, wasting away in his absence.",
 "modern": "தோழியே, நீ வாழ்க! என் தலைவன் மீது ஏதேனும் தவறு இருக்கிறது என்று கூறினால், அது உண்மையில் தவறா? ஒரு தவறும் இல்லை. இறந்த வழிப்போக்கர்களின் மீது இலைகளால் மூடப்பட்ட கல் பதுக்கைகள், பெரிய நல்ல யானைகளுக்கு நிழல் தரும் இடமாக விளங்கும் வெம்மையான பாலைவனத்திற்குச் சென்ற என் தலைவனுக்கு, என்னுடைய பருத்த மென்மையான தோள்கள் எளிதாகக் கைவிடப்பட்டு மெலிந்து போயின.",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by the heroine to her confidante (tōḻi) who blames the hero for leaving her to go to the desert in search of wealth. The heroine defends her lover, saying that he is not at fault. She describes the harsh desert (pālai tiṇai) where the only shade available for the great elephants is the stone cairns (patukkai) of dead travelers covered with dry leaves. This grim image highlights the extreme danger and difficulty of the path he has taken. She concludes that the fault lies not with him, but with her own broad, soft shoulders which were so 'easy' (eḷiya) to be abandoned and have now wasted away in grief.",
